From 8743e05a0b8585b9ceffe658dfa17bdefeb04e01 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aiden Barrett <aidenbarrett101@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 00:15:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] Stop the hashed order depending on the host, and
 reproduce bd6ca03 exactly

The Windows identity failure had THREE causes, not two. The previous commit
closes the two that move no identity. This closes the third, which cannot be
closed outside the hash, and which is why that commit does not on its own turn
Windows green.

WHAT WAS WRONG

  _files() ended in `sorted(out)`, sorting Path objects. `PurePath.__lt__`
  compares the host flavour's normcase form. On POSIX that is the string as
  written. On Windows it is `str(path).lower()`, with backslash separators and
  case folded. So:

      POSIX    .../cpp_project/CMakeLists.txt, README.md, include/sandbox/...
      Windows  .../cpp_project/CMakeLists.txt, include/sandbox/..., README.md

  'R' is 0x52 and sorts before 'i' 0x69; 'r' is 0x72 and sorts after it. The
  identical tree, byte for byte, hashed two different ways depending on which
  machine read it.

  The earlier `.as_posix()` fix corrected the key each file contributes. It did
  not correct the order the keys are contributed in, and the order is just as
  load-bearing.

THE PROOF, WHICH IS THE POINT

  The observed CI value is now reproduced exactly rather than reasoned about.
  Computed on the tree at 5d729478, from Linux, by applying each candidate
  transformation to the real bytes:

      canonical order, LF       e16b2f01c4fd4ec4623b4588cffa064fe270ca5ee52b1e290762f8ae91e2566b
      canonical order, CRLF     6ba842bd27204895d9511a04106a81079c82af846429e638d69995f4d64e657c
      Windows order,   LF       6c0b0cde2c9a8fd5fe1eb4eb34cdf314ee4fe68d545124271920d75e367cc734
      Windows order,   CRLF     bd6ca03ea18c0c12ddf3ab190aac50bb36d6fdf2d2eaafa3d4c296dc905f0dc6

  The last one is GitHub Actions run 50, to all seven digits that were
  reported. Three further things follow from an exact match that no amount of
  argument would have given:

  - The Windows hashed file SET is identical to the Linux one, 76 files. That
    kills the competing hypothesis that rglob or _SKIP behaved differently
    there, which was the other live explanation.
  - The CRLF was tree-wide, not fixture-only. Both the checkout and the
    generator were producing it, and the fixture-only and non-fixture-only
    candidates (ca3777ac and 7dcbec8e) are excluded by measurement.
  - `.gitattributes` plus the generator fix land on 6c0b0cde, not e16b2f01.
    Necessary, and demonstrably not sufficient.

  Four earlier candidate hashes, computed before the ordering cause was found,
  all failed to match. Reporting a miss rather than shipping the nearest
  plausible story is what turned this up.

THE FIX

  Sort on `_key`, the same canonical repository path the digest records. The
  order becomes a property of the repository rather than of the host reading
  it, and the sort key and the hashed key are now literally the same function,
  so they cannot drift apart again.

  One regression, which fails on every host rather than only the one that had
  the bug. It asserts the invariant, asserts that source_sha256 is exactly the
  canonical-order digest, asserts the tree still contains a pair the two
  orderings disagree on (so the test cannot go vacuous without saying so), and
  asserts the two orderings produce different digests (so the invariant is
  load-bearing rather than decorative).

WHY INSTRUMENT.json MOVES, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A GENERATION

  provenance.py is inside the hashed surface, so any correct fix to it changes
  source_sha256. There is no version of this that leaves the declared identity
  alone; the alternative is leaving Windows unable to reproduce the instrument
  at all.

      source_sha256       e16b2f01c4fd4ec4623b4588cffa064fe270ca5ee52b1e290762f8ae91e2566b
                       -> a7a1c268d82e969f821111322c3ea9064685e03d7e5e9f488461a81e5c563e9b

      base_prompt_sha256  f1fb88236b18c2aa21d396a91cdc878274005cb47d687b1442811c6a740f1559
                          UNCHANGED

  base_prompt_sha256 is what ends a generation, and it has not moved: nothing
  the model is told, and nothing its answer is judged by, changes here. The
  generation stands at 2 and archived rows remain re-scorable across this
  commit, subject to rescore_dataset.py per row as usual. Nothing is
  reinterpreted and no frozen artifact is touched.

  The change is provenance-only. It cannot alter a measured outcome; it alters
  only which machines agree on what the instrument is.

  Historical hashes quoted in .gitattributes and in the line-ending regression
  are relabelled as historical in the same commit, so no stale number is left
  sitting in the tree looking current.

